[
    {
        "content": "<p>Hello! <br>\nI'm sorry if this question has been asked &amp; answered before (&amp; maybe this is a bit of a beginner question). I came across the need to turn an equality of types intro a function allowing to pass objects through this equality, and came up with this :</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Mathlib</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Logic</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Equiv</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Defs</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">FunFromTypeEq</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">T1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">T2</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">eq</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">T1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">T2</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">T1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">T2</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">intro</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"bp\">;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">rw</span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">eq</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">at</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"bp\">;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">apply</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">TheseAreInverse</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">T1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">T2</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">eq</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">T1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">T2</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">T1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≃</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">T2</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">mk</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">FunFromTypeEq</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">eq</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">FunFromTypeEq</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">eq</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">symm</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"k\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">unfold</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Function</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">LeftInverse</span><span class=\"bp\">;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">intro</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"bp\">;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">unfold</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">FunFromTypeEq</span><span class=\"bp\">;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">simp</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"k\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">unfold</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Function</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">RightInverse</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Function</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">LeftInverse</span><span class=\"bp\">;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">intro</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"bp\">;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">unfold</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">FunFromTypeEq</span><span class=\"bp\">;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">simp</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>Should this small hack be authorized? Is there any problem with writing this? (Lean does not complain, but being able to use the power of rewrite in this context seems very powerful). I did not manage to find anything relating to turning type equalities to equivalences in Lean, was I just unlucky in finding some \"official\" conversion?</p>",
        "id": 609200720,
        "sender_full_name": "William Hasley",
        "timestamp": 1783595689
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Yes, this is definitely authorized, and exists as <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=cast#doc\">docs#cast</a> and <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=Equiv.cast#doc\">docs#Equiv.cast</a></p>",
        "id": 609201088,
        "sender_full_name": "Floris van Doorn",
        "timestamp": 1783595830
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Though often we try to design definitions/libraries so that we minimize the needs of such casts, since having a lot around can make proving things about these functions painful.</p>",
        "id": 609201220,
        "sender_full_name": "Floris van Doorn",
        "timestamp": 1783595874
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>To find declarations like this, you can use <a href=\"https://loogle.lean-lang.org/\">#loogle</a></p>",
        "id": 609201463,
        "sender_full_name": "Floris van Doorn",
        "timestamp": 1783595959
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"644391\">@loogle</span> ?a = ?b → ?a ≃ ?b</p>",
        "id": 609201476,
        "sender_full_name": "Floris van Doorn",
        "timestamp": 1783595966
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span aria-label=\"search\" class=\"emoji emoji-1f50d\" role=\"img\" title=\"search\">:search:</span> <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/Logic/Equiv/Defs.html#Equiv.cast\">Equiv.cast</a></p>",
        "id": 609201484,
        "sender_full_name": "loogle",
        "timestamp": 1783595969
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>or more simply:</p>",
        "id": 609201565,
        "sender_full_name": "Floris van Doorn",
        "timestamp": 1783595988
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"644391\">@loogle</span> _ = _ → _ ≃ _</p>",
        "id": 609201577,
        "sender_full_name": "Floris van Doorn",
        "timestamp": 1783595993
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span aria-label=\"search\" class=\"emoji emoji-1f50d\" role=\"img\" title=\"search\">:search:</span> <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/Logic/Equiv/Defs.html#Equiv.cast\">Equiv.cast</a>, <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/Logic/Equiv/Basic.html#Equiv.subtypeEquivProp\">Equiv.subtypeEquivProp</a>, and <a href=\"https://loogle.lean-lang.org/?q=_%20%3D%20_%20%E2%86%92%20_%20%E2%89%83%20_\">56 more</a></p>",
        "id": 609201586,
        "sender_full_name": "loogle",
        "timestamp": 1783595996
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Ahh, I see, I'm much more convinced with the usage of the induction principle!<br>\nThank you very much for your answer! (and sorry for the question, I guess I did not search far enough)</p>",
